3, so count_greater is 1.\n\nFor element 2: elements before are [1,3]. 1 is less than 2 (count_greater +=1), 3 is greater than 2 (count_less +=1). So count_less is 1, count_greater is 1.\n\nSo total count_less is 0 + 0 +1 =1. count_greater is 0 +1 +1=2. Not equal, so returns False.\n\nAnother example: list [1,2,3]. For each element:\n\nelement 1: count_less 0, count_greater 0.\n\nelement 2: elements before are [1], which is less than 2. So count_less is 0 (no elements before greater than 2). count_greater is 1 (element 1 is less than 2). So count_greater is 1.\n\nelement 3: elements before are [1,2], both less than 3. So count_less is 0, count_greater is 2.\n\nTotal count_less: 0+0+0=0. count_greater: 0+1+2=3. Not equal. So returns False.\n\nHmm, so in these cases, the function returns False. But what if the list is [2,1,3,4].\n\nLet\'s compute:\n\nelement 2: elements before none. count_less 0, count_greater 0.\n\nelement 1: elements before [2], which is greater than 1. count_less 1, count_greater 0.\n\nelement 3: elements before [2,1]. 2 is greater than 3? No, 2 is less than 3. 1 is less than 3. So count_less is 0. count_greater is 2 (elements before that are 2 and 1, both less than 3). So count_greater is 2.\n\nelement 4: elements before are [2,1,3]. 2 <4, 1 <4, 3<4. So count_less is 0. count_greater is 3.\n\nTotal count_less is 0 +1 +0 +0 =1. count_greater is 0 +0 +2 +3 =5. Not equal. So returns False.\n\nAnother example: list [1,3,2,4].\n\nelement 1: 0.\n\nelement 3: elements before [1], which is less than 3. count_less 0. count_greater 1.\n\nelement 2: elements before [1,3]. 1 is less than 2 (count_greater +=1), 3 is greater than 2 (count_less +=1). So count_less 1, count_greater 1.\n\nelement 4: elements before [1,3,2]. 1,3,2. 1 is less than 4, 3 is less than 4, 2 is less than 4. So count_less 0. count_greater 3.\n\nTotal count_less 0 +0 +1 +0 =1'
