[36m(TaskRunner pid=607832)[0m code='\nTo solve the problem of calculating the last digit of the factorial of a given integer, we need to break the problem into manageable parts. The key insight is that for integers greater than or equal to 5, the factorial (n!) will always end in at least one zero, making the last digit 0. For smaller numbers (0 ≤ n ≤ 4), we compute the factorial directly and return the last digit.\n\nThe solution involves:\n1. Handling edge cases (n < 0).\n2. Returning 1 for n = 0 (since 0! = 1).\n3. Returning 0 for n ≥ 5 due to the trailing zeros in factorials.\n4. Computing the factorial for n < 5 and returning the last digit.\n\nHere is the implementation:\n'
